NAME
    lua - execute Lua 5.5 source code

SYNOPSIS
    lua [OPTIONS] [SCRIPT [ARG...]]
    lua --version

DESCRIPTION
    Runs Lua 5.5.1 source from one or more -e arguments, a filesystem file, or
    standard input. -l MODULE loads a module before the script and stores its
    result in the MODULE global; a -version suffix is omitted from the global
    name. -l NAME=MODULE selects an explicit global. -E ignores
    environment-based Lua configuration, -W enables warnings, and -v prints the
    version. Unless -E is present, LUA_INIT_5_5 or its LUA_INIT fallback runs
    before command-line actions. Script arguments are available both as chunk
    varargs and at positive indices in the global arg table. arg[0] is the
    script name and preceding interpreter options occupy negative indices. With
    no source argument, reads standard input. At an interactive prompt that
    reads one script from the session terminal until Ctrl-D rather than
    starting a prompt loop; interactive -i/REPL operation is not present.

    The base, package, coroutine, debug, io, math, os, string, table, and utf8
    libraries are present. loadfile, dofile, require, package.preload, and the
    Lua-file module searcher use the declared read-only filesystem capability.
    The default module path searches /share/lua/5.5, /share/lua, and the current
    directory. In-memory and file-backed binary chunks are accepted.

    io provides standard streams, temporary in-memory files, and filesystem
    files, including lines, formatted reads and writes, append/update modes,
    flushing, and seeking. Lua has explicit filesystem-read and
    filesystem-mutation authority. Writable files are buffered in the Lua heap
    and replaced through TROE's streamed mutation service when flushed or
    closed. file:setvbuf selects full, line, or unbuffered flush behavior;
    unbuffered writes are visible through the filesystem before close.
    io.popen uses TROE's owner-scoped process-launch and bounded-pipe services in
    both read and write modes. C dynamic modules remain unavailable because TROE
    has no dynamic-loader service.

    os.clock follows Lua semantics and returns process CPU seconds charged while
    the application executes unprivileged code. Kernel services, waiting, and
    descheduled time are excluded. os.time() returns whole Unix seconds from
    TROE's wall clock. os.time(table) and os.date implement the standard calendar
    interface in UTC; TROE has no per-process timezone database or
    daylight-saving setting. Wall time is not monotonic and may step when corrected. For an
    end-to-end benchmark long enough to tolerate one-second resolution, use
    os.time() before and after the workload. That elapsed result includes kernel
    service, waiting, and descheduling time. os.exit terminates the Lua
    application with an optional Unix-style low-8-bit command status and cannot
    be caught with pcall. Its second argument
    follows Lua semantics: false (the default) skips Lua-state closing, while
    true closes the state and runs its closing work before termination. TROE
    reclaims the application heap after either form. os.remove deletes regular
    files, symbolic links, or empty directories. os.rename atomically renames
    files, symbolic links, or directories within one writable filesystem
    provider. Cross-provider renames and existing destinations fail without
    changing either path.
    KEX invocations carry a bounded immutable launch environment composed by
    the launcher, not by the application. os.getenv reads exactly the entries
    the launcher supplied, resolves PWD from the invocation directory, and
    returns nil for every other name; nothing is supplied from inside lua
    itself. An interactive session supplies HOME, PATH, TMPDIR, SHELL, USER,
    and LOGNAME. Direct child processes inherit that same canonical environment
    with PWD updated to the launch directory. One name carries at most one
    value: a duplicate name is refused when the environment is encoded, so a
    launcher replaces an inherited entry rather than appending to it.

    LUA_PATH_5_5 and LUA_CPATH_5_5 take precedence over LUA_PATH and LUA_CPATH,
    and LUA_INIT_5_5 takes precedence over LUA_INIT. -E ignores all of them
    before the libraries open, so module paths keep their built-in defaults and
    no initialization chunk runs. -E does not change os.getenv, which is not
    Lua configuration. os.execute parses one bounded direct TROE command, waits
    for the child, and returns Lua's standard success, termination kind, and
    status tuple. Its command may use a bare /bin name or an explicit relative
    or absolute KEX path. Quoted arguments and backslash escapes are supported.
    Shell operators, expansion, and redirection are rejected because TROE does
    not delegate the session-owned shell-script capability to nested processes.
    io.popen uses the same command syntax. The fixed C locale is the only
    supported locale. Filesystem and process failures use stable errno values,
    including 18 for cross-provider rename, 30 for read-only media, and 39 for
    nonempty directories.

    Filesystem, environment, process, errno, calendar, decimal, math, and
    C-locale algorithms come from the shared no_std TROE KEX runtime. The
    SDK-owned C facade performs correctly rounded binary64 formatting for Lua's
    %f, %e, and %g conversions. The Lua image statically links that code. The
    runtime is neither a complete libc nor dynamically linked.

    Lua's standard math.random generator is seeded automatically from TROE's
    kernel CSPRNG on every launch. It is suitable for games, simulations,
    shuffling, and sampling, but remains a pseudorandom math API rather than a
    cryptographic byte interface. Explicit math.randomseed arguments retain
    normal Lua reproducibility.

    Lua uses an initial TLSF arena and capability-backed private mappings for
    larger allocations. Configured process/system ceilings and available owned
    frames bound growth without a legacy 1 MiB application limit. Exhaustion
    raises a normal protected Lua memory error and cannot access ambient host
    resources.

EXAMPLES
    Working example programs are shipped as architecture-independent data:

        lua /share/lua/examples/language.lua
        lua /share/lua/examples/numbers.lua
        lua /share/lua/examples/system.lua

    A portable compute and allocation benchmark can run unchanged under TROE
    and Alpine Lua 5.5:

        lua /share/lua/benchmark.lua 1 5 troe

    Measure a sufficiently long end-to-end workload in whole wall seconds:

        local t0 = os.time()
        workload()
        print(os.time() - t0)

    Read them with cat, copy one to /tmp, and adapt it there. The immutable
    originals remain available in the recovery root.
